const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Editorial and Quality Support","serviceType":"Editing and editorial QC","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/medical-writing/editorial-quality-support-service/","description":"Independent editing and QC of documents written elsewhere — the level named before the quote, in the EFA, Chicago and Editors Canada vocabularies.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What types of documents do you support?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Journal manuscripts of every design; systematic reviews and meta-analyses; case reports; theses and dissertations; grant applications and research proposals; book chapters and scholarly reports; regulatory and clinical documents; and institutional or publisher work at volume. Across medicine and the health sciences, the life sciences, engineering and computing, psychology and the social sciences, economics and management, and the humanities. We also take documents that have already been edited somewhere else — that is frequently the most useful thing we do, and section four of the sample report on this page is exactly that job."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you guarantee acceptance in journals?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The answer that was already on this page is the right one and we have kept it: while we bring a manuscript up to high editorial and formatting standards, we do not guarantee journal acceptance. Acceptance is an editorial decision, made by people we have no relationship with, about science we did not do. It is worth adding what the published evidence says about why editing helps less than the market implies: in the best-documented analysis of reviewer comments, the leading reasons for rejection were statistical problems, overinterpretation, inappropriate instrumentation and sample size, with “text difficult to follow” ranking fifth. Editing reaches the fifth-ranked problem well and the first four not at all. Note also that no publisher-affiliated editing service claims otherwise — the strongest promise any of them makes is a free re-edit or a refund, not an outcome."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What similarity score is acceptable?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"There is no acceptable score, because the score is not a measure of plagiarism. Turnitin’s own guidance says so in almost those words: “plagiarism cannot be determined by the similarity score alone”, the number is “simply the percentage of text in a submission that matches other sources”, and there is “no single ‘acceptable’ or ‘perfect’ Similarity score”. A correctly quoted, correctly cited passage raises the number exactly as a copied one does. Nor is there a published threshold. COPE states that it “does not recommend using a specific percentage of duplication or overlap as a benchmark”. Elsevier tells its editors a high score does not necessarily indicate plagiarised text. Springer Nature publishes bands for editors — screen quickly above 10%, check carefully above 20% — and says explicitly that individual source scores matter more than the overall index. The “under 15%” figure that circulates widely traces to no publisher, no guideline and no primary source we could find. What we do is read every match and tell you which need a citation, which are your own earlier work and need handling as text recycling, and which are a real problem."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you make sure my paper passes an AI detector?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and we would not take your money for trying. Seven GPT detectors tested on human-written TOEFL essays produced an average 61.3% false-positive rate, and 19.8% of those essays were called AI-generated by every detector, while essays by native-English school students were classified almost perfectly (Liang et al., Patterns, 2023). The tools key on text perplexity, so they penalise writing with less lexical variety — which means they systematically penalise the authors most likely to buy reassurance about them. Turnitin’s own claims have moved a long way from the “98% confidence” of its 2023 launch: it now publishes no headline accuracy figure, does not surface results below 20%, warns that submissions under 300 words are unreliable, and states that a score “should not be used as the sole basis for adverse actions”. COPE’s September 2025 guidance is the line to hold: detection tools “should not be relied upon in isolation”. What we will do is help you write an accurate declaration of whatever AI you actually used, in the form your publisher requires."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which level of editing do I need?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Use the chooser above, then send the document and we will confirm it by editing a thousand words free. The short version: if the problem is typographical, proofreading. If it is grammar, terminology and references, copyediting. If the sentences are correct and hard to read, line editing — the level most authors need and least often buy. If the structure or the argument is wrong, substantive editing. If the document needs rebuilding rather than improving, developmental editing, which starts with a plan you approve before any rewriting. The reason this question is hard is that there is no industry-standard taxonomy: the Editorial Freelancers Association names four services, the Chicago Manual of Style splits manuscript editing into mechanical and substantive at 2.49 and 2.50 and treats line editing as an earlier separate pass, and Editors Canada’s Professional Editorial Standards 2024 defines four levels under different names again. They do not map onto each other, which is why two honest quotes can differ fourfold."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who will edit my document?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"An editor working in your subject area, matched to the document type as well as the discipline — a thesis editor and a regulatory editor are different people, and so are a copyeditor and a developmental editor. Then a second reader who did not do the editing, on every job at every level, because an editor re-reading their own work reads what they intended rather than what is on the page. You are told who is doing it, and the same editor stays with the document through revisions and is available when the reviewers or your supervisor come back. Where a document needs a level we cannot staff properly in your field, we say so and decline rather than assigning somebody who will learn the subject on your manuscript."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will you change what my paper says?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not without asking. The rule we work to is that an editor may change how a sentence reads without asking and may not change what it claims without asking — and in a discussion section those two things conflict constantly. Where they do, the claim wins and the query goes to you, with the estimate or the evidence the sentence rests on attached. The sample report on this page includes a real example of the failure: another provider turned “was associated with” into “led to” in an abstract. It reads better and it asserts causation from an observational cohort, which is the objection that design always attracts. It was made without a query, so the authors never saw the decision being taken. Every one of our queries, and the reasoning behind it, is in a log you keep."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you provide a certificate of editing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We provide written confirmation of exactly what was done, at what level, on what date — which is useful, and which your institution may require for a declaration. We do not market it as an editing certificate, because certificates in this market are sold as though they carry editorial weight and they do not. No publisher we checked requires one, treats one as bearing on a decision, or rejects a paper for lacking one. The only publisher-side mention we found is MDPI, which says that if you use a service that issues a confirmation certificate you may send a copy to the editorial office — permissive, with no stated effect. Publishers’ own editing arms sell certificates too, and are careful about what they promise: Springer Nature’s guarantee alongside its certificate is a free re-edit if a paper is rejected for the English alone, not acceptance. Treat the “Scopus-accepted” and “Web of Science ready” certificates advertised in this market with suspicion: neither organisation operates any such scheme."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you edit my thesis, and do I have to declare it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Sometimes, and the answer is your institution’s rather than ours. Universities differ sharply: some permit language editing within stated limits, some require a declaration naming the editor and the work done, some prohibit third-party assistance in assessed work entirely. Send us the regulations before you send us the thesis. Where help is permitted we work inside the limits — which usually means language and consistency but not structure or argument — and give you written confirmation of exactly what was done so your declaration is accurate. Where it is prohibited, we decline, and we will not help anyone work around it. For journal articles the position is different and simpler: we found no publisher that prohibits professional editing, and all the major ones require only that it be acknowledged, with Springer Nature and BMC also asking for the funding source of the assistance."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you handle confidentiality?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A non-disclosure agreement is signed before any material moves, and editors and project staff are bound by internal confidentiality agreements as well. Unpublished data, drafts, decision letters and supervisor correspondence are held under the UK GDPR on access-controlled systems within a quality management system certified to ISO 9001:2015, certificate H3CR9TCU20251212INDQM10N, kept only as long as the project needs them and never used to train anything. We do not reuse your text on anyone else’s project, and we do not publish your work as a sample — every sample on this site is redacted or composite. A document Pubrica holds is confidential whoever wrote it, so the sample on this page names neither the client nor the earlier editor, and never will."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Medical writing","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/medical-writing/editorial-quality-support-service/"}]}];

